Pitch type predictor.  I just like baseball and think this would be fun, though not exactly groundbreaking.  The what: given game context, predict the probability of each pitch type and location for the next pitch.  The Why: with how good pitching is today, hitters often find themselves sitting on specific pitches in specific areas that they feel they can drive vs. reacting to 100 MPH fastballs that tunnel with 90 MPH sliders and sweepers with 35 inches of horizontal break.  Better prediction means better hitting (not that they don’t have much more sophisticated models for this).  The How: millions of labeled pitches are free through pybaseball (MLB’s Statcast).  Would treat an at-bat as a sequence of pitches, like the bigram case, and try a small transformer or RNN over the pitch history.  The model would have two outputs, a probability over pitch types and a probability over location (the classic 3x3 grid plus another layer around the shadow of the zone).  Given those two are correlated it’d most simply be location given type.  We could evaluate against a baseline of middle-middle fastball only, though the more interesting comparison would be with league average pitch selection and location and then holding out games by each pitcher and comparing them to the expected output… would need to think more about how to do that best.  